{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>et My sabbaths you shall keep, etc.</b> One must know why He returned to say a second time what He commanded at the beginning of the portion (Leviticus 19:3). And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, in Torat Kohanim said, and this is their language: ״Perhaps the building of the Temple pushes aside the Sabbath? Scripture says: אֶת שַׁבְּתֹתַי תִּשְׁמֹרוּ,״ and so forth — end quote. Their words, of blessed memory, take us out of the difficulty of the juxtaposition, but the difficulty of the doubling remains in its place: for he should have written ״וּמִקְדָּשִׁי תִּירָאוּ״ adjacent to the statement ״וְאֶת שַׁבְּתֹתַי תִּשְׁמֹרוּ״ that was said at the beginning of the portion, and there would have been no need to say a second time ״וְאֶת שַׁבְּתֹתַי״ etc." "<b>And it appears</b> that this commandment is connected with what precedes it, for since HaShem commanded the father not to profane his daughter by making her prostitute, sometimes it may occur that the daughter of her own accord will profane her own honor and her father's honor to prostitute, and the father's hand is not in the middle. Therefore HaShem commanded the father that he should keep watch so that the daughter not prostitute, for everything depends on the one who brings forth [children]. And there are three causes that can bring about the birth of an evil nature in those born: the first, if at the time of conjugal union he intends toward the gratification of the act of intercourse and not for the sake of the Creator's commandment to maintain the species — this will generate in the offspring an overpowering of evil. The second kind [of cause] proceeds from the mother, meaning from the fact that the woman's deeds are not good — ״one woman follows the way of another״ (Ketubot 63a). The third, if the parents — father or mother — had thoughts of the aspect of evil while their mind was diverted, like the deed of King Hezekiah, upon him be peace (Sanhedrin 104a), whose thought caused the birth of one unfit, though they were foundations of the world." "Against the first cause He said <b>אֶת שַׁבְּתֹתַי תִּשְׁמֹרוּ</b>, in accordance with their statement in the Zohar (2:88b) on the verse ״to the eunuchs who keep My sabbaths״ (Isaiah 56:4), that they are the righteous who castrate themselves and do not consort with their wives except on Sabbath eve, and they draw down for their children holy souls — end quote. See that one who wishes to beget a son of a pure soul must castrate himself and restrain the force of desire, and act only for the commanded purpose from HaShem blessed be He. And this is the statement of HaShem to remove a prostituting spirit from his house: <b>אֶת שַׁבְּתֹתַי תִּשְׁמֹרוּ</b> — to be deliberate with them, and thereby you will beget a holy soul that abhors prostitution. And against the second cause, which is the apprehension from the mother's side, He said <b>וּמִקְדָּשִׁי תִּירָאוּ</b>, a warning to hold the sanctuary in awe, and thereby the woman will guard herself lest she be brought to the sanctuary to be made to drink the bitter waters. And against the third cause He juxtaposed to this the warning <b>do not turn to the ovot and to the yid'oni</b>, which are the aspect of impurity that desires and craves to become attached to the pure person." "And the word <b>ovot</b> [relates to] the language of avah [desire], in accordance with His saying (Deuteronomy 23:6) ״and He did not avah [desire] HaShem.״ And the allusion of <b>yid'oni</b> is to say that it should not become known to him, for knowledge of impurity compels a person to become attached to it, like the nature of the lowly that desires to become attached to what is above it and sought after." "<b>And He said: I am YHWH your Elohim</b>, He comes to give the reason for the drawing of an evil soul from the cause of thought, for since HaShem is our Elohim — meaning, He dwells within the soul of Israel — when a person thinks thoughts of the aspect of evil, the force of holiness withdraws and nothing presents itself before him to draw down except the portion of evil. It is found that anyone who does not guard himself against the three causes is profaning his daughter, etc."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {3 "The lemma reads אוֹבוֹת (ovot) but the translation follows the text, which links it to the root אוה (to desire), citing Deut. 23:6 where the root appears; the wordplay depends on this derivation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:47.015177176Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}